    "field:rack::dsp::Decimator::inIndex": {
      "finding": "P0: the in_index setter accepts any value from 0 to OVERSAMPLE*(QUALITY-1) (native/bindings/dsp/resampler.cpp:225-229). That range only checks the next copy. Rack's Decimator::process (Rack-SDK include/dsp/resampler.hpp:125-129) does memcpy(&inBuffer[inIndex], in, OVERSAMPLE) and then inIndex = (inIndex + OVERSAMPLE) % (OVERSAMPLE*QUALITY), so Rack only ever leaves inIndex at multiples of OVERSAMPLE. With a value that is not a multiple, a later call writes past inBuffer. Example: Decimator[4, 8], in_index = 1. After 7 process() calls inIndex is 29, and the 8th call copies into elements 29..32 of a 32-element array. Element 32 is kernel[0] (kernel[0..3] for float_4), so Python can silently corrupt memory, which is undefined behaviour. This also makes docs/reference/dsp.md:563-565 ('keep in_index within the range Rack's code keeps it in') and the manifest note wrong. tests/dsp/in_rack_basic/test_resampling.py:261-263 only checks 28 and 29. Smallest fix: in the setter, also require in_index % OVERSAMPLE == 0 (ValueError); add a test that in_index = 1 raises; update the doc and the note.",
      "verdict": "finding"
    },
